            <ArticleTitle>Mild Traumatic Brain Injury in a High School Football Player With Familial Hemiplegic Migraine: A Case Report.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1934-1482(16)31114-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pmrj.2017.07.081</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Mild traumatic brain injury is a major concern in young athletes, with an estimated 1.6-3.8 million reported concussions in the United States annually. Familial hemiplegic migraine is a rare autosomal-dominant condition characterized by sporadic episodes of transient unilateral motor weakness that may begin at any age. We present a case of a 17-year-old boy with a history of familial hemiplegic migraine who suffered prolonged symptoms after a mild traumatic brain injury during sports participation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LEVEL OF EVIDENCE" NlmCategory="METHODS">To be determined.</AbstractText>
